{"name":"Tropical Waterfall","hex":"#bee7e2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tropical","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to the tropics, the equatorial region between 23 degrees north and 23 degrees south.","From, or similar to, a hot, humid climate."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tropical plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tropical"},{"term":"waterfall","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A flow of water over the edge of a cliff.","A waterfall-like outpouring of liquid, smoke, etc."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fall like a waterfall.","To drink (something) from a container by pouring it from a height so as not to touch one's lips to the rim."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/waterfall"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tropics","blocks":[{"text":"The tropics are the region of Earth surrounding the equator, where the Sun may shine directly overhead. This contrasts with the temperate or polar regions of Earth, where the Sun can never be directly overhead. Because of Earth's axial tilt, the width of the tropics is twice the tilt. The tropics are also referred to as the tropical zone and the torrid zone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/World_map_indicating_tropics_and_subtropics.png/330px-World_map_indicating_tropics_and_subtropics.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropics"},{"title":"Waterfall","blocks":[{"text":"A waterfall is any point in a river or stream where water flows over a vertical drop or a series of steep drops. Waterfalls also occur where meltwater drops over the edge \nof a tabular iceberg or ice shelf. Waterfalls can be formed in several ways, but the most common method of formation is that a river courses over a top layer of resistant bedrock before falling onto softer rock, which erodes faster, leading to an increasingly high fall. Waterfalls have been studied for their impact on species living in and around them."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/Cascada_Dynjandi%2C_Vestfir%C3%B0ir%2C_Islandia%2C_2014-08-14%2C_DD_136-138_HDR.JPG/330px-Cascada_Dynjandi%2C_Vestfir%C3%B0ir%2C_Islandia%2C_2014-08-14%2C_DD_136-138_HDR.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterfall"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Bora_Bora.jpg/500px-Bora_Bora.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Bora_Bora.jpg/500px-Bora_Bora.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Aerial view of Bora Bora in French Polynesia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABora_Bora.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg/500px-Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg/1280px-Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg/500px-Kota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tropical sunset over the sea in Kota Kinabalu in Malaysia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKota_Kinabalu_by_Dale_Preston.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Monthly_zonal_mean_precipitation.png/500px-Monthly_zonal_mean_precipitation.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/33/Monthly_zonal_mean_precipita